  {
   "k": "j6k4di",
   "en": "Strategies and guides"
  },
  {
   "k": "1345498",
   "en": "The below is ⟦0⟧ player suggested strategies for ⟦1⟧. Bear in mind, due to the dynamic nature of the game, it may unfold differently for other players.",
   "frags": 2
  },
  {
   "k": "uicvoj",
   "en": "The biggest challenge is low recruitable population. You can't switch out of 'limited conscription' until you get into the war with the Soviet Union, and, even with all population focuses, 'limited conscription' is just barely enough for 32 divisions of 4 battalions + support units. You need at least that many just to fully man the border and to prevent the Soviet Union from doing a naval invasion straight to Helsinki."
  },
  {
   "k": "kskvan",
   "en": "It makes sense to edit the templates to remove some battalions (you start with 6 battalions of infantry in the infantry template.) You can add them back after switching to \"extensive conscription\" in 1940."
  },
  {
   "k": "1d8ln6o",
   "en": "If you don't mind slight cheating, AI Soviet Union will gladly accept a non-aggression pact with you as late as February 1939 (possibly even later.) You just need +100 improved opinion and a spy doing \"diplomatic pressure\". Then, once the AI does the focus for the Winter War, they can't declare on you because of the non-aggression pact, and, by the 12 month mark, their casus belli expires. You can then either sit and do nothing (boring) or do the focus for the Continuation War (which still works, even if the Soviet Union does not fight the Winter War.) You will then go to war in the summer of 1941, and you should be able to get Leningrad, Karelia and Murmansk while the German Reich does most of the fighting."
  },
  {
   "k": "1ibivbu",
   "en": "Nordic Union (non-aligned)"
  },
  {
   "k": "xljumj",
   "en": "Replace Svinhufvud. Ban fascism and communism. Join Anti-Comintern and do focuses that would grant extra recruitable population and factories."
  },
